From fe51e8730fe1e75f5987adb6f72633982a36cf1b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Portal <116828772+Retrorerr@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:57:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] backends/anland: Composite on select, without catch-up
 repaints

The consumer selects a buffer at the display's vsync and presents what
KWin renders into it, so it already paces the output. The RenderLoop's
own vblank prediction started each composite about a vblank after the
select, minus its expected render time, and two vblanks after it when two
selects came close together: a frame of latency on every frame, and a
dropped frame each time. A presentation safety margin of at least two
vblanks, the cap on the expected compositing time, makes it composite at
once.

A buffer that missed damage asked for a full-output repaint until it was
selected again. That repaint was new damage for the other buffers, so
once anything changed every later frame repainted the whole output. Each
buffer already accumulates the damage it missed, and doBeginFrame
repaints it the next time the buffer is selected, so the catch-up repaint
is dropped. A frame with nothing to repaint is not rendered, and the
render-done message now tells the consumer so (REFRESH_DONE_SKIPPED): the
selected buffer is stale, and the consumer keeps presenting the last one.
A frame whose buffer was never selected drops its fence instead of
sending it with the next frame. Without a native fence, glFinish makes
the frame complete before it is presented.

Input events carry the CLOCK_MONOTONIC time Android sampled them
(INPUT_TYPE_EVENT_TIME, written together with its event). Stamping events
when KWin read them made their spacing jitter by as much as a composite
takes, which clients read as changes in finger speed. Implausible stamps
fall back to the read time, and event times never go backwards.

The X11 touchpad fling steps once per output frame, so each frame scrolls
by exactly the time since the last one. Its free-running 8 ms timer beat
against the vsync, giving some frames two steps and others none; the
timer now steps only when no frames come.
